UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 16, Lesson 1: What makes a good friend', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA friend says, ''If you don''t give me your snack, I won''t be your friend.'' Is this respectful? Use two friendship qualities in your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nNot respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a description of a made-up good friend with five qualities and a real-life style example for each. Add two signs that a friendship is not respectful and name one trusted adult you could tell.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a description of a made-up good friend with five qualities and a real-life style example for each. Add two signs that a friendship is not respectful and name one trusted adult you could tell. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example: Ari lists friend qualities with real examples. Kind: shared his pencil. Honest: said my drawing needed a title but liked the colours. Reliable: turned up to help at recess. Respectful: accepted ''no'' when I wanted to play alone. Each quality has an example.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. Correction: Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult.","Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and demonstrate\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the reasoning\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Leave someone out because the game felt easier that way."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Leave someone out because the game felt easier that way.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new situation\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening.","Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"A friend says, ''If you don''t give me your snack, I won''t be your friend.'' Is thi\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\". Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening.","Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a sh\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named cues, rules or choices.","question":"Describe or practise a fresh situation that uses the central skill. Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Example: Ari lists friend qualities with real examples. Kind: shared his pencil. Honest: said my drawing needed a title but liked the colours. Reliable: turned up to help at recess. Respectful: accepted ''no'' when I wanted to play alone. Each quality has an example.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the choice or strategy, not just make the answer longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus a corrected choice and a reason linked to safety, health or respect.","question":"Name one likely misconception or unsafe choice for this topic, correct it and say why the correction is safer, healthier or more respectful.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. Correction: Not respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w16-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a good friend.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use a good friend with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify kindness and explain why it matters.","I can practise honesty independently on a new prompt.","I can notice respect needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ari lists friend qualities with real examples. Kind: shared his pencil. Honest: said my drawing needed a title but liked the colours. Reliable: turned up to help at recess. Respectful: accepted ''no'' when I wanted to play alone. Each quality has an example.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA friend says, ''If you don''t give me your snack, I won''t be your friend.'' Is this respectful? Use two friendship qualities in your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nNot respectful: it pressures and threatens. A respectful friend accepts a ''no'', which shows respect and kindness. You can say ''No thanks'' and tell a trusted adult if it keeps happening.","explain":"A good friend is kind, honest, reliable and respectful. Good friends listen, share, include others, say sorry and help each other. Friendships can change, and people can have many types of friends. Respectful friends accept ''no'' and do not pressure, threaten or leave people out. Anyone who makes you feel unsafe is not acting like a good friend, and you can tell a trusted adult.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking friends must always agree and do everything together. Friends can disagree, have different interests and still be kind and respectful. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Good friend qualities.","caption":"Name an example.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x